{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>From the animal.</b> Our Sages of blessed memory derived in Torat Kohanim to include hybrids from a goat and a ewe, or vice versa, and from the phrase ״כָּל חֵלֶב״ they included the koy, which is the offspring of a goat and a deer. And see what I have written in my work Pri To'ar on Yoreh De'ah, siman 16." "<b>Every eater of fat.</b> In Torat Kohanim they derived to include the fat of unconsecrated animals (chulin), so that you should not err by saying: ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב מִמֶּנָּה אִשֶּׁה לַה׳״ — Scripture spoke specifically of the fat of consecrated animals. Therefore Scripture says ״כָּל חֵלֶב״, and this inclusion, if it does not apply to those who eat, apply it to that which is eaten." "It is difficult for me: how could one err to say specifically the fat of consecrated animals? For it says ״וְחֵלֶב נְבֵלָה יֵעָשֶׂה לְכָל מְלָאכָה״ — as for fats of consecrated animals, are they permitted to a private individual? Did they not say in כיצד צולין (Pesachim 82b): if it was slaughtered and found to be a treifah, it must go out to the place of burning — for since it is written (Leviticus 6:23) ״בַּקֹּדֶשׁ בָּאֵשׁ תִּשָּׂרֵף״, this teaches that all disqualifications in consecrated food require burning? And if so, from His saying ״וְחֵלֶב נְבֵלָה וְחֵלֶב טְרֵפָה יֵעָשֶׂה לְכָל מְלָאכָה״ you know that Scripture did not prohibit only those of consecrated animals, and it should not have needed to say ״כָּל אֹכֵל״. And one may say that the primary teaching of the Tanna from ״כָּל אֹכֵל חֵלֶב״ is regarding the liability of karet, for without the word ״כָּל״ — although I would have derived from the verse ״יֵעָשֶׂה לְכָל מְלָאכָה וְאָכֹל לֹא תֹאכְלֻהוּ״ — I would have said it is not absolute, and not karet, and that karet applies only to the fat of consecrated animals." "<b>All fat of ox and sheep and goat you shall not eat.</b> And its saying ox, sheep, and goat — they derived in Torat Kohanim that this comes to exclude an impure animal, a wild beast, and a bird. And in Chulin (116a) Rav Mari said to Rav Zevid: if the fat-tail (alyah) is called חֵלֶב, should it be forbidden for consumption? He said to him: About you Scripture says ״חֵלֶב שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״ — something that is equivalent in ox, sheep, and goat; this far [and no further]. And it is difficult: did they not need ox, sheep, and goat to exclude an impure animal and a bird and a wild beast, which would have been derived by a fortiori argument (kal vachomer) for the prohibition, as stated there in Torat Kohanim — therefore Scripture says ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״? Perhaps, since these three are themselves excluded by the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב מִמֶּנָּה אִשֶּׁה לַה׳״ — a wild beast is excluded, an impure animal is excluded, a bird which is not entirely offered is excluded. Or perhaps, after Scripture excluded an impure animal and a wild beast, Scripture thereby revealed that one should not reason by a fortiori argument from blood, for impure animals and wild beasts prove it. And so you will find that Rashi wrote in Chulin (117a) in his commentary on the Mishnah, which states that it applies only to pure animals — for it is written ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב מִמֶּנָּה אִשֶּׁה לַה׳״. And see the following verse." "<b>From the animal.</b> They derived in Torat Kohanim to include unconsecrated animals (chulin) with blemishes, so that you should not say chulin are analogous to consecrated animals — therefore Scripture says ״מִן הַבְּהֵמָה״ to include even those with blemishes. And although Scripture explicitly prohibited the fat of a nevelah and a treifah even though they are forbidden on the altar — granted regarding a treifah we may say according to the Rambam's view (Issurei Mizbe'ach, ch. 2) that there is no biblical prohibition for the altar but only because of (Malachi 1:8) ״הַקְרִיבֵהוּ נָא לְפֶחָתֶךָ״, and although in Torat Kohanim (chapter 1, halakhah 2) they derived to forbid it from the verse — it is merely an asmakhta, as the Kesef Mishnah wrote there — but a nevelah is disqualified from the altar, for ״וְשָׁחַט״ is written regarding all offerings. And one may say that regarding the matter of karet, the Tanna retracts, as I wrote above." "<b>That one would offer.</b> They said in Torat Kohanim: What is the reason for saying ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״? Fat that is like it — i.e., fit to be offered upon the altar; this excludes the fat of the flanks, which is not fit to be offered. End quote. Now according to this reasoning, which excludes impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl from the phrase ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״, there would have been no need to say ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״, for it was only necessary to exclude the fat of the flanks." "And it is difficult according to Rav Zevid, who derived from ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״ the permissibility of the fat of the tail, and from ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״ to exclude impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl — from where does he derive the permissibility of the fat of the flanks? Also, according to the Tanna who derived from ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״ to exclude impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl — from where does he derive the permissibility of the fat of the tail? And one may answer for Rav Zevid that the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״ excludes impure beasts, wild animals, fowl, etc., and also the fat of the flanks — for anything that is not offered to HaShem is included in the exclusion. And the Tanna of Torat Kohanim also derives from the phrase ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״ the exclusion of the fat of the tail, for if Scripture came only to exclude impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl, it need only have said שׁוֹר וָשֶׂה, and when something that is subject to the prohibition of blood is excluded — do we not then apply a fortiori? And after all, why would it have been necessary to exclude it, since I would have derived it by a fortiori from blood? Through one exclusion — whether of an impure beast or a wild animal or a fowl — the a fortiori is subsumed, so there was no need except to teach about the fat of the tail, which is not uniform across all of them, that it is permitted." "But the mind is still not settled by this: why would Rav Zevid disagree with the Tanna of Torat Kohanim, who excludes impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl from ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״, while he excludes them from ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב מִמֶּנָּה לַה׳״? Furthermore, the Tanna revealed his position that the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״ etc. would not suffice to derive from it the exclusion of impure beasts, wild animals, fowl, and the fat of the flanks — as evidenced by his dividing them into two separate exclusions. Therefore the correct view in my eyes is that according to everyone, the exclusion of impure beasts, wild animals, and fowl is only from the phrase ״שׁוֹר וְכֶשֶׂב וָעֵז״; and this derivation would have been valid even had it said merely שׁוֹר וָשֶׂה, and by specifying כֶשֶׂב and עֵז it was to exclude the fat of the tail; and the phrase ״אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיב״ comes to exclude the fat of the flanks — and not as Rashi explained (Ḥulin 117a), which I cited above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:27.375804089Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}